{"author":{"id":"2eecc7d99b502c158aacb0868dca9358610edc02724d21fe49b0196402d3364e"},"ops":[{"type":1,"timestamp":1785185876,"nonce":"Ndi3wFESctY5KiQKSKg7rWCzdQE=","title":"ADR-0062 part 4: zone preference has no merge story with ADR-0055 placement overlays","message":"ADR-0062 part 4 says Scarab expresses a **preferred** zone affinity on a Step Pod so it lands near its Export. `affinity` currently appears **nowhere** in the Rust sources, and ADR-0055 applies a `PlacementProfile` as an opaque pod-spec **block** the operator owns — so whichever writer runs last replaces the other's affinity wholesale.\n\nADR-0062 explicitly promises not to do this: it rejects hard-pinning partly because it would \"silently amend the placement overlays ADR-0055 makes the operator's to own\". A preference that clobbers an operator's affinity does the same thing more quietly.\n\nDefine the merge. A data-locality preference and an operator's placement policy both have to survive, and when they genuinely conflict the operator's must win — Scarab's preference is an optimisation and theirs is policy.\n\n\n- [ ] Scarab's zone preference and an operator-supplied affinity compose rather than overwrite\n- [ ] When they conflict irreconcilably the operator wins, and the Run says the preference was dropped — never silently\n- [ ] `preferredDuringScheduling`, never `required`: a Pod must still schedule when its zone is out of capacity (spot is the stated environment)\n- [ ] A test with a `PlacementProfile` that sets its own affinity, proving neither side is lost\n\n\n- 0ea908b (s6)","files":null}]}